Seborrheic Dermatitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Greasy yellow scales, Sebaceous gland distribution, Erythematous plaques

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-039
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning and scaling facial rash causing extreme distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting an explosive, highly pruritic, and burning erythematous eruption across the central face and scalp over the past 24 hours. The patient expresses severe psychological distress and anxiety regarding a potential acute systemic autoimmune flare (e.g., cutaneous lupus) or a severe allergic contact dermatitis. Urgent on-site clinical assessment is required to perform a comprehensive dermatological evaluation, definitively rule out early facial cellulitis or acute systemic disease, and immediately prescribe targeted topical therapies to suppress the massive inflammatory response. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of escalating patient panic and uncontrolled facial inflammation, satisfying the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My face is burning, completely covered in greasy yellow flakes, and the itching is driving me mad.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms follow a chronic and relapsing course but have acutely flared over the past 48 hours following a period of extreme physiological stress at work and a sudden drop in ambient temperature (cold, dry weather). The patient reports the rapid development of erythematous plaques, accompanied by copious dandruff flaking from the scalp. The rash has a striking predilection for sebaceous-rich areas, exhibiting a symmetrical distribution on the face specifically localized to the nasolabial folds and eyebrows. Mild to moderate pruritus and a burning sensation are present. Denies fever, joint pain, shortness of breath, or the application of any new facial cosmetics.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly distracted and distressed by facial disfigurement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute situational anxiety.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 132/84 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distressed, frequently rubbing the scalp and face.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A clinical diagnosis with a negative fungal culture ruling out primary dermatophytosis would assist in confirming the diagnosis in atypical cases, but is not acutely indicated for this classic presentation.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals distinct erythematous plaques with a prominent overlay of greasy yellow scales. The lesions strictly follow a sebaceous gland distribution, heavily involving the bilateral nasolabial folds, glabella, medial eyebrows, and the anterior scalp margin. The distribution is perfectly symmetrical. There is an absolute absence of deeper dermal involvement, induration, or fluctuance. No malar rash crossing the nasal bridge sparing the nasolabial folds. The remainder of the skin, including the neck and extensor surfaces, is completely clear.
Environmental Observations: Residence is heavily heated with dry indoor air.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has aggressively scrubbed the face with harsh alkaline soaps in an attempt to remove the flakes, profoundly worsening the erythema.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Seborrheic Dermatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Malar Rash) - Definitively ruled out as the current erythema explicitly involves the nasolabial folds (which lupus typically spares) and features prominent greasy scaling rather than a fixed, flat erythematous flush.
Psoriasis - Unlikely given the lack of thick, silvery micaceous scale, absence of nail pitting, and lack of extensor surface involvement (elbows/knees).
Facial Cellulitis - Ruled out by the lack of unilateral spreading warmth, absence of deep dermal induration, lack of fever, and the chronic/relapsing timeline.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive facial eruption generated immense patient panic, mimicking more severe systemic or acute infectious dermatoses. Urgent home evaluation was necessary to physically examine the specific distribution and scale morphology, definitively excluding acute facial cellulitis and acute lupus erythematosus. By confirming a severe flare of benign seborrheic dermatitis triggered by stress and environmental factors, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was averted. Providing immediate reassurance, potent topical antifungals, and mild corticosteroids addressed the severe localized distress, fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ketoconazole 2% cream to be applied to affected facial areas twice daily until clear. Recommended a short, 5-day course of Hydrocortisone 1% cream applied sparingly once daily to the face to rapidly suppress the acute inflammatory burning sensation. Advised Ketoconazole 2% shampoo for the scalp.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the fungal overgrowth from thriving, strictly wash your face with a gentle, pH-balanced cleanser rather than harsh soaps. Alkaline soaps destroy the skin's acidic barrier, creating an open environment where the natural yeast on your skin multiplies rapidly, directly triggering the massive inflammatory flare and greasy scaling.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Consider a cool-mist humidifier to counteract dry indoor heating.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 14 days to monitor resolution and establish a long-term maintenance skin-care regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness becomes intensely hot, deep, and painful, if it spreads rapidly over one cheek, or if you develop a high fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; while highly characteristic, non-responsive facial rashes require formal dermatological review.
Clinical Course: The intense burning and redness will subside within 3 to 5 days of commencing the topical therapies. The scaling may take 1 to 2 weeks to completely resolve. This condition is chronic and requires ongoing, intermittent maintenance therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical review if the steroid cream causes thinning of the skin or if the rash completely fails to respond to the medicated creams after two weeks.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is violently overreacting to a normal, harmless yeast that lives in the oily parts of everyone's skin. The antifungal cream directly kills the yeast, removing the trigger, while the mild steroid cream acts as a temporary fire extinguisher to immediately stop the burning and redness.
Actionable Steps: Do not aggressively scrub the flakes away with a washcloth or loofah; friction heavily exacerbates the inflammation. Gently massage the medicated shampoo into the scalp and leave it completely undisturbed for 5 minutes before rinsing, allowing the chemical enough time to penetrate the scale.

